</answer> 

One way to think about this assignment is as follows. Field_2 (threat 0.35) and Field_3 (threat 0.15) are already at full protection (4 and 2 drones needed respectively), so we want to preserve that. Field_4 (threat 0.05) currently has 2 protecting drones but needs 3 for full protection, while Field_1 (threat 0.11) is not protected at all. Since partial protection (fewer than the full required number) does little to make the birds flee, and because Field_1’s threat is higher than Field_4’s, it makes sense to “steal” one drone from Field_4 and reassign it to Field_1. That way Field_1 gets some (even if partial) deterrence while Field_4, with a lower threat level, still has one drone rather than none. With this redistribution the fields break down as follows:

• Field_1 gets 1 drone (needs 4, but even partial protection is better than none)
• Field_2 remains at full protection with 4 drones
• Field_3 remains fully covered with 2 drones 
• Field_4 is left with 1 drone (from 2 originally, but its threat is low)

This grouping minimizes the overall damage by ensuring that the most threatening fields (Field_2 and Field_3) remain fully covered and that at least some protection is given to the other fields.
